This is really solid work! You've clearly understood JDBC and Spring Boot integration well. The progression from your previous assignments is impressive.
What you absolutely nailed:
Complete CRUD implementation - All four operations (add, update, view, delete) are properly implemented with correct SQL statements. Your repository pattern is clean and follows good practices.
Proper Spring Boot setup - The configuration looks great. H2 database setup, schema initialization, and dependency injection are all correctly configured. Using Lombok was a smart choice too.
Good separation of concerns - Your layering is spot on: Repository handles data access, Service handles business logic, and the main application just orchestrates everything. This is exactly how it should be structured.
Practical testing approach - Using CommandLineRunner to test your CRUD operations is perfect for this kind of task. Shows all the operations working in sequence.
Minor areas for improvement:
Your schema is more complex than what you're using - You've got this nice ERD with authors, assessments, and ratings, but you're only working with courses. Not a problem for the assignment, but in real projects you'd want to implement the full domain or simplify the schema to match.
RowMapper could be simpler - Since you're using Lombok, you could potentially use Spring's BeanPropertyRowMapper instead of writing your own mapper. Though honestly, custom mappers give you more control.
Error handling - Your repository methods don't handle cases where a course might not exist (like in findById). In production, you'd want some exception handling there.
What impressed me:
The way you structured your project packages, the clean separation between layers, and how you demonstrated all the CRUD operations working together. You've really gotten the hang of Spring's ecosystem.
Your SQL is clean, your entity model makes sense, and the whole thing just works. This feels like code I'd be comfortable seeing in a real project (with a bit more error handling).
You're definitely getting the hang of enterprise Java development patterns!
